  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLow pressure continues to evolve off the southern New England\ncoast. Forcing has struggled to consolidate so far, as\nconvective influences have really dominated. Gradually the\nexpectation is that a broad band of precip will set up over the\nfar southern zones of the forecast area. Because precip has been\nso light, temps have not fallen much below the upper 30s. So\nsnow has been hard to come by except for at the highest\nelevations. As the precip shield fills in thru sunrise I do\nanticipate that some light snow will occur over the higher\nterrain of the Upper Valley into the Monadnocks. Ultimately this\nis looking more like a couple of inches for peaks like the\nMonadnocks and Sunapee. The biggest change in guidance over the\nlast 24 hours has been to rapidly turn off precip during the\nfirst half of today. Much of the area north of LEB to PWM look\nto be dry thru the entire system. A dry, northerly breeze will\nhelp to clear things out today but it will remain chilly.\n\nCool and dry continues thru the weekend as high pressure builds\nback in. Daytime temps will likely be a couple degrees below\nnormal, but overnight ideal radiational cooling conditions will\nsee readings well below normal. Heading towards midweek temps\nwill begin to moderate and above normal temps may return to the\nregion.",
